# 1) Treatment vs. Utilization

The `treatment` variable indicates whether an individual was offered treatment. The `ohp_all_ever_survey` variable indicates whether an individual was ever enrolled in medicaid. One can be in either group with or without also being in the other group. That is to say that untreated individuals may also access medicaid, and treated individuals may not access medicaid. `treatment` can also be described as "intent to treat," as it is the variable assigned randomly and used in our experimental setting.

# 2-3) Balance Table

```{r balance_table, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results='asis', cache=TRUE}
pacman::p_load(tidyverse, magrittr, tableone, stargazer, fixest, sandwich, modelsummary, gtsummary, systemfit, knitr)

ohp <- read.csv("ohp.csv")

# Balance Table
pre_survey_vars <- c(
  "age_inp", 
  "dep_dx_pre_lottery", 
  "dia_dx_pre_lottery",
  "edu_inp",
  "hbp_dx_pre_lottery",
  "hispanic_inp",
  "numhh_list",
  "race_black_inp",
  "race_white_inp"
)

models <- lapply(pre_survey_vars, function(var) {
  feols(as.formula(paste(var, "~ treatment")), data = ohp, vcov = "hetero")
})
names(models) <- pre_survey_vars

modelsummary(
  models,
  title = "Balance Table",
  output = "gt",  # changed to gt for compatibility
  shape = model ~ term,
  stars = TRUE
)

```

Individuals in the treatment group appear to be slightly older and slightly more likely to be diagnosed with depression. These differences are small and not very significant, but are worth noting. For the most part, the groups appear balanced. 

# 4) Compliance 
```{r compliance, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results='asis', cache=TRUE}
comp <- lm(data = ohp, ohp_all_ever_survey ~ treatment)
se_comp <- sqrt(diag(vcovHC(comp)))

stargazer(
  comp,
  se = list(se_comp),
  type = "html"
)

```

Treatment has an effect significant at the 1% level. Medicaid enrollment increases by 25pp in the treated group compared to the control group.

