# 6) Model Comparison

Health outcomes are each measured in different units, so they must be normalized before comparison.

Because only two variables show a significant treatment effect, it is only necessary to compare those two to one another.

```{r sur,  echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results='asis', cache=TRUE}
ohp %<>%
  mutate(
    dep_z = scale(dep_dx_post_lottery)[,1],
    dia_z = scale(dia_dx_post_lottery)[,1],
    doc_z = scale(doc_num_mod_inp)[,1],
    hbp_z = scale(hbp_dx_post_lottery)[,1],
    bp_z = scale(bp_sar_inp)[,1]
    )

health_outcomes_z <- c(
  "dep_z",
  "dia_z",
  "doc_z",
  "hbp_z",
  "bp_z"
)

models_norm <- lapply(health_outcomes_z, function(var) {
  feols(as.formula(paste(var, "~ treatment")), data = ohp, vcov = "hetero")
})

names(models_norm) <- c("Depression Dx", "Diabetes Dx", "Doctors Visits", "High BP Dx", "BP Average")

modelsummary(
  models_norm,
  title = "ITT Effects, Normalized",
  output = "gt",  # changed to gt for compatibility
  shape = term ~ model,
  stars = TRUE
)

# Effect Comparison: Compare dia_z and doc_z

eq_dia <- dia_z ~ treatment
eq_doc <- doc_z ~ treatment

system <- list(dia = eq_dia,
               doc = eq_doc)

sur_model <- systemfit(system, method = "SUR", data = ohp)
```

```{r hyp,  echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE,  cache=TRUE}

linearHypothesis(sur_model, "dia_treatment = doc_treatment")

```

While I am struggling to interpret the output of the `linearHypothesis` function, it appears to suggest a p value of $p = 0.1714$. This fails to reject the null hypothesis that both effects are equivalent. In other words, this suggests that the ITT effect on diabetes diagnoses and doctors visits are statistically indistinguishable from one another.

