# 8) ATET

Average Treatment Effect on the Treated (ATET) is the treatment effect divided by the compliance rate, giving the effect of treatment on compliant individuals. This prevents the treatment effect from being "watered down" by individuals who did not accept treatment. Estimates are normalized.
```{r atet,  echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results='asis', cache=TRUE}
itt <- c(
"Depression Dx" = 0.021,
"Diabetes Dx" = 0.068,
"Doctor Visits" = 0.033,
"High BP Dx" = 0.010,
"BP Average" = -0.004)

compliance <- 0.254

atet <- itt / compliance

kable(atet)

```

# 9) Attrition

Attrition bias can occur if attrition is systematically different among treatment and control groups. This is important to watch for in a study of this nature. For example, if every unhealthy person in the control group dies before the final survey, then this can give the impression that outcomes for individuals in the control group improve. To know if attrition bias is a concern, we would need to perform some analysis comparing individuals who appear in the final survey with those who do not.

# 10) Abstract

The Oregon Health Plan Experiment seeks to expand Medicaid access through randomly awarded eligibility. The random assignment of eligibility creates an ideal environment to measure the effect of Medicaid on health outcomes. In this paper, we employ a linear regression model to estimate the Intent To Treat Effect (ITT) and the Average Treatment Effect on the Treated (ATET) on health outcomes related to disease diagnoses, number of doctor visits, and bodily health measurements. No significant ITT effect was found for depression diagnoses, high blood pressure diagnoses, or average blood pressure. Diabetes diagnoses were shown to have a positive ITT effect of 0.068 (0.268 ATET) standard deviations, significant at the 0.1% level. Doctors visits were also shown to have a positive ITT effect of 0.033 (0.130 ATET) standard deviations, though this effect was only significant at the 10% level. We were unable to show that these two effects are distinguishable from one another. The compliance rate among treated individuals was 25.4%. The results suggest increased access and utilization of medical resources, but fail to suggest any positive health effects. 








